* Linux Terminal

## 💻 Commands Practiced

### 1. Basic Scan

```bash
nmap localhost
```

This performs a basic scan of the local system.

### 2. Service and Version Detection

```bash
nmap -sV localhost
```

This attempts to identify the services and their versions running on open ports.

### 3. Scan Ports 1–1000

```bash
nmap -p 1-1000 localhost
```

This scans ports from 1 to 1000.

### 4. Operating System Detection

```bash
nmap -O localhost
```

This attempts to identify the operating system of the target.

## 📊 Understanding Ports

A port is a logical communication endpoint used by network services.

Examples:

| Port | Common Service |
| ---- | -------------- |
| 22   | SSH            |
| 53   | DNS            |
| 80   | HTTP           |
| 443  | HTTPS          |
| 21   | FTP            |
| 25   | SMTP           |

## 🔎 Understanding Scan Results

### Open

A service is actively listening on the port.

### Closed

The port is reachable, but there is no service currently listening.

### Filtered

Nmap cannot determine whether the port is open because filtering, such as a firewall, is blocking the response.

